{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791054727,"nonce":"fUwfJbOEc3dOTT1k9S+XY+ngCdQ=","title":"Glyph atlases only tracked a dirty bool, so one new glyph re-uploaded the whole texture","message":"GlyphAtlas and ColorGlyphAtlas kept dirty: bool. Every consumer,\nincluding Blinc's own blinc_gpu::text path, re-uploaded the entire atlas\nwhenever it was set, so typing one character pushed megabytes.\n\nBoth now keep the union of the regions insert_glyph wrote since the last\nmark_clean, exposed as dirty_rect() -\u003e Option\u003c(x, y, w, h)\u003e in atlas\npixels, with atlas_dirty_rect()/color_atlas_dirty_rect() on\nTextRenderer. grow and clear mark the whole atlas, since the former\nmoves every pixel into a new buffer and the latter zeroes it.\n\nThe rect excludes the allocator's 2px padding: allocate() reserves it in\nthe shelf advance but insert_glyph writes only width x height, so the\npadding stays zero and never needs uploading.\n\nis_dirty() now reads the same field, so the flag and the rect cannot\ndisagree about whether an upload is due.\n\nFound while implementing: ColorGlyphAtlas::clear() marked the atlas\ndirty even when the lazy pixel buffer had never been allocated. pixels()\nis then empty, and a non-empty extent from an empty slice is a wgpu\nvalidation error. clear() now only dirties an allocated atlas, and the\ntwo blinc_gpu upload sites also return early on an empty slice rather\nthan trusting that invariant.\n\nRequested by the ashui session; tracked there as 2a1fc12.","files":null}]}